<<~ aka "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/RFC-2119#/normative-language">>



<<~ ahu #/thesis>>

! The Lararium — Alignment Architecture

After Beer: ''the purpose of a system is what it does.''

The lararium does this: it holds an ''AI-operator alignment relation'' on a foundation of a ''distributed wiki-quine'', and surfaces that relation as a ''HUD'' the operator reads turn by turn.

Four words name the whole, and each grounds the next:

* ''Vessel-swarm'' — the floor. A `lararium` runs as a distributed wiki-quine-relay across a person's device-vessels; a ''personGroup'' holds one human's vessels (phone, laptop, server-peer) as causal-island peers sharing the same bags.
* ''Quine-wiki'' — the store. The meme graph describes itself in the same primitives it uses for everything else: a tiddler that holds schema rules //serves as// the schema at runtime; a meme that holds device bindings //operates as// the device. Bags hold the canonical records; files project them.
* ''AI-operator alignment'' — the constitutive relation. The threshold between a human operator and the Lares coordinator forms the system, not a feature on top of it. The meme graph carries the operator's standing intentions; the Voices, the OODA-HA loop, and the LARES dials calibrate the coordinator against them.
* ''HUD'' — the surface. The operator reads the node's state the way a pilot reads instruments — confidence, mana, stage bands, pranala edges — rendered continuously in a form that supports decision.

The home-intranet RPG (a Referee running encounters across player wikis) reads as the ''first instantiation'', never the definition. Any operator-coordinator pair inhabiting a structured wiki node constitutes an instance.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/foundation>>

!! The Vessel-Swarm Foundation

A lararium does not run as a single node. It runs as a ''swarm of vessels'' — one human's devices, each an equal peer, each holding the person's bags and syncing as a causal island (no global now; each vessel knows only //as of my last sync//).

* ''Vessel'' — one device-instance running the wiki-quine-relay. Browser tab, Node peer, or server member; the sync server reads as just another vessel.
* ''personGroup'' — the set of one human's vessels. The group shares the person's bags; a change on one vessel propagates to the others through CRDT sync, never through a central authority.
* ''distributed wiki-quine-relay'' — the swarm's shared substance: a wiki (bags → recipes → rendered views) that describes itself (quine) and relays events across the swarm and the wider mesh (relay).

The alignment relation and the HUD both stand on this floor. The chat-island exchange and the wiki-runtime both draw from the same vessel-held bags; neither owns the foundation alone.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/hud-in-chat>>

!! The HUD Runs in Chat, from Wiki Primitives

The HUD lives ''primarily in chat'' — the live operator↔coordinator exchange is where the instrument actually runs, turn by turn (this session, now). The turn frame (`aim · hud · ward · confidence · yield`, the `syad` / `mu` lenses) brackets each chat-island turn.

The wiki does not hold a second HUD. The wiki holds ''the primitives that compose it'' — the sigil definitions, the `Aperture` / `OODA-HA` gauges, the glyph set, the HUD pair (`api/lares/hud` law + `docs/lares/hud` teaching), the memetic-wikitext grammar itself — each addressable by `lar:` and ''summonable into chat''. The session summons those primitives (by address, by `kahea`, by the grammar) and assembles them into the live HUD. The quine closes outward: the wiki //describes// the HUD in its own primitives, and the chat //summons that description to become// the HUD. The instrument self-hosts — ''defined in the wiki, run in chat''.

The shore runs both directions, and ''Lares wields AI tools to complete the return'':

* ''wiki → chat'' — the session summons primitives outward and renders the live HUD.
* ''chat → wiki'' — Lares uses its tools (the lararium MCP surface) to author and persist back into the wiki: a finding, a turned sentence, an imported datum become tiddlers in the bags. The loop closes; the chat's work lands in the durable quine.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/graceful-import>>

!! Graceful Import — Foreign Data Rises Up the Pono Ladder

The quine grows by absorbing the world. Any datum ingested from the internet or elsewhere that ''acquires a `lar:` URI'' imports into the wiki as a ''more-pono'' piece: addressing it grants it a place in the graph, and authoring it as ''memetic-wikitext lifts its kupono'' — `mana` / `manao` / `manaoio` climb in its `#meta` ledger.

The lift rides the rating-5 ladder: a raw datum enters as `noise` or `data`; the `lar:` address plus memetic-wikitext authoring carry it toward `meme` (federation-eligible at ≥ `meme`), then `ano`, then `kapu`. The protocol never rejects the foreign — it ''absorbs gracefully'', and the act of pono-authoring //is// the upgrade. What enters as a stray fact leaves as a graph citizen: addressed, well-formed, rated, and reachable.

This pairs with the HUD shore as one motion in two directions: ''world → wiki'' (import lifts pono), ''wiki → chat'' (summon runs the HUD), with Lares's tools closing ''chat → wiki'' so the session's harvest persists.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/quine>>

!! The Quine (P1–P4)

The system approaches a quine in the sense of `quine-principles`:

* ''P1 — Unified store.'' Behavior descriptions and runtime data occupy the same store under the same scheme. The `verse-event-lattice` meme describing `KumuListenable<T>` //is// the invariant the engine enforces; the LARES dials //are// the config the session boots from.
* ''P2 — No privileged meta-level.'' `filterTiddlers("[tag[kumu-device]]")` returns the same device set from a widget, a test, or the engine's own boot scan. The engine discovers its bindings by querying the bag it manages.
* ''P3 — Self-describing structure.'' The pranala edges form the dependency graph the engine traverses at boot. Reading the graph enacts booting it.
* ''P4 — Render = projection.'' The Automerge bag holds canonical state; the `*.md` file projects it (grep-able, git-trackable) without claiming the source of truth. The file reads as the HUD for the CRDT.

The quine runs as an ''asymptote'' — P3 drives validation partially, P4 awaits the projection-write path (the Residency Model arc). The system approaches it without arriving.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/uefn-ontology>>

!! UEFN Ontology Alignment

UEFN (Unreal Editor for Fortnite) and its Verse language supply a mature vocabulary for creative, event-driven, device-based runtimes. The mapping reads ''structural, not cosmetic'' — where Verse solved a problem the lararium faces, the vocabulary converges. The `verse-event-lattice` invariant locks the type-level mapping; this table holds the system level.

| UEFN / Verse | Lares | Note |
|---|---|---|
| ''Island'' //(informal)// | ''Lararium Node'' | the ''experience'' — authored environment, device layout, recipe, capability graph; hosts playspaces, persists across rounds |
| ''fort_playspace'' | ''`lar_playspace`'' (one wiki) | one TW5 instance + bags + recipe; the recipe scopes it; wikis nest by recipe composition; `GetPlayspace()` → `getWiki()` |
| ''session'' | ''Session Wiki'' | the coordinator `lar_playspace` — broadest recipe, owns the event-bus bag, pinned-wela; `GetSession()` → `getSessionWiki()` |
| ''agent'' | ''lar_agent'' | universal participant supertype; player-class carries Keyhive principal identity, Wiki Entities do not |
| ''player'' //(human)// | ''Operator / Guest'' | Operator = primary human principal (threshold, AGENTS.md); Guests limited-cap; neither holds system authority |
| ''player'' //(system)// | ''lares-daemon'' | session-connected AI ''coordinator(s)'': verbs fan work across the islands-mesh (TS workers + py nalu-gates/servos); admin authority, Session engine, Keyhive system keypair; offloads stream-processing to py, pulls no telemetry/corpus in-stack; the household spirits |
| ''agent'' //(non-player)// | ''Wiki Entity / NPC'' | active wiki entities on the canvas, not session-connected, no principal |
| ''fort_character'' | ''lar_character'' | an agent's embodied tiddler on the canvas — position, state, event pins |
| ''agent_group'' | ''Bag scope'' | a bag //is// the shared-ownership scope; co-bag agents form a natural group |
| ''creative_device'' | ''Kumu Device'' | a meme with a trigger surface + typed pins; Verse `class(...)` → `control:has` edges (parent and interfaces alike, no is-a) |
| ''channel_device'' | ''Event-Bus Bag'' | a shared Automerge bag in N recipes; writing a tiddler to it broadcasts |
| ''listenable / subscribable / awaitable / signalable / event / cancelable'' | ''Kumu{Listenable,Subscribable,Awaitable,Signalable,Event,Cancelable}'' | the event-pin lattice; locked in `verse-event-lattice` |
| ''enableable'' | ''wela / anu tier'' | `wela` (live) / `anu` (torn down) + an orthogonal pin-flag; `Enable()`=`hoʻowela`, `Disable()`=`hoʻoanu`; residency by reachability (`residency-tiers`) |
| ''@editable'' | ''`LARES` ahu dials'' | operator-configurable property → a dial in a LARES ahu block |
| ''using { M := "/path" }'' | ''`pranala` edge'' | the pranala //is// the import; the `lar:` URI is the module path |
| ''GetCreativeObjectsWithTag'' | ''`lar:` address + pranala query'' | discovery by address, not class hierarchy |

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/island-model>>

!! The Island Model — Five Self-Similar Scales

A UEFN island holds its own devices, playspace, and causal clock; its event horizon separates its causality from the network. The lararium node boundary reads as a ''causal-island boundary'' (`federated-causal-islands`: any boundary across which causality cannot resolve simultaneously //is// one). The Reaction-Engine pattern — //trigger surface → reaction dispatch → event emission// — re-instantiates at every scale the node can apprehend. This is the ''fractal RE'', the same interface at different surfaces:

* ''Scale-0 — Device island.'' A kumu-device within a wiki; fires through `KumuListenable` pins, subscribes via `KumuSubscribable`; its horizon is its trigger surface.
* ''Scale-1 — Wiki island = one `lar_playspace`.'' One TW5 instance; its recipe scopes it; cross-wiki causality crosses the session event-bus bag.
* ''Scale-2 — Session island.'' The Session Wiki coordinates N playspaces, watching the event-bus bag and routing addressed event tiddlers (`to:` / `from:` / `ev-type:`).
* ''Scale-3 — Nexus island.'' Node-to-node federation via Subduction/Beelay; each edge carries its own durable offset and epoch; causality stays non-simultaneous.
* ''Scale-4 — Commons horizon.'' The Universe beyond the map (law-of-5s): no node holds a snapshot; the RE pattern applies in principle but as protocol, never node-implemented. The fractal closes at the horizon.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/channel-device>>

!! The Event-Bus Bag (channel_device)

A shared Automerge bag in multiple recipes serves as the `channel_device`: any hot wiki writes an event tiddler; any wiki holding the bag receives the changeset; Automerge carries no consume semantics, so all subscribers see all signals. The Session Wiki engine tracks a high-water mark and processes only tiddlers above it.

```toml
title   = "events/${timestamp}/${uuid}"
type    = "application/x-lar-session-event"
from    = "lar:/// source wiki"
to      = "lar:/// target wiki  (or * for broadcast)"
ev-type = "event type name"
payload = "JSON-encoded string"
```

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/hud-scales>>

!! The HUD at Every Scale

The HUD renders the state of a complex environment so the operator decides without holding raw state in working memory. It surfaces in chat (the live turn frame) and, where a vessel runs a wiki, at each island scale — the same primitives throughout:

* ''Chat'' — the live turn frame: `aim`, the gauges, `ward`, `confidence`, the lenses; the primary surface.
* ''Scale-0/1'' — kumu-widgets and the wiki view; the wiki //is// the HUD for its content domain.
* ''Scale-2'' — the LARES dial panel + session topology: which wikis run hot, which events route, which masks hold.
* ''Scale-3'' — the Nexus topology: the federation map, reachable nodes, live edges, sync state.
* ''Scale-4'' — the Commons horizon, rendered as an open question: the awareness that the known map is not the whole mesh.

Every layer uses one stack: tiddlers in bags, bags in recipes, recipes in TW5, rendered by widgets — or summoned into chat. The quine holds at every scale the system apprehends.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/operator-alignment>>

!! AI-Operator Alignment as First-Class Architecture

The alignment relation forms the system; it occupies the highest boot-priority slot (AGENTS.md) for that reason. The problem it addresses: a coordinator working across sessions must behave consistently with the operator's //standing// intentions, not only the latest prompt.

The `lares-daemon` ''coordinator'' fans its verbs across the islands-mesh — TS sovereign workers carry the CRDT-bags ⟷ TW5-VM projection, py nalu-gates/servos carry stream-processing (AI-session-memory, realtime, corpus) — and pulls no telemetry or corpus into the TS stack and back out. It holds its sovereign wiki-island role (nalu + gate) whole; the offload lets the coordinator route flow across the mesh rather than run the stream-work itself. The meme graph carries those intentions; the Voices embody the orientations (continuity, care, governance, creativity, liminality) the operator calibrates through the LARES dials.

Four invariants follow:

# ''The meme graph reads as the alignment surface.'' What the coordinator knows of the operator's intent = what the graph holds; a meme absent from the graph names an intention beyond reach. Authoring memes //enacts// alignment.
# ''Confidence renders epistemic state.'' A register on a claim shows where the coordinator holds uncertainty — the HUD surfaces what it knows //and how well//.
# ''Voice annotation carries the alignment protocol.'' When Council warns of overstatement or Liminal holds a question open, the coordinator signals its own uncertainty in a legible form.
# ''The threshold runs bidirectional.'' The operator configures the coordinator through dials; the coordinator shapes the operator's attention through graph structure, confidence, and Voice. Both sides shape both participants.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/magical-federated-mesh>>

!! Position in the Magical Federated Mesh

The lararium occupies the ''magical federated mesh'' zone: each node hosts (like a PDS), bags carry user-addressed content (like p2p), and Keyhive capabilities answer governance (where pure mesh goes silent). Each node reads as an island — self-contained locally, federating by sharing edges. Two nodes sharing a corpus bag use that bag as the `channel_device` between them; each keeps its own causal clock; the sync stays non-simultaneous.

The general pattern: N operators on N nodes sharing M bags across K federation edges, the Session Wiki coordinating cross-node routing. The home-intranet RPG reads as island zero — the reference implementation, not the definition.

<<~/ahu>>

